\begin{abstract}
This paper presents a unified simulation framework for dark matter and
dark energy within the Universe-Wide Simulation \& Ingestion Engine
(UW-SIE v2.0). The Dark Matter / Dark Energy Coupled Engine (DMDE-USE)
is stress-tested under extreme cosmological conditions, including
high-density filament collisions and accelerated expansion parameter
surges, while maintaining stable gravitational potential convergence and
spacetime manifold integrity.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Dark matter and dark energy are the dominant components of the universe's
energy budget, yet remain poorly understood at a fundamental level.
Numerical cosmology typically treats them via separate modules: N-body
solvers for dark matter and background expansion models for dark energy.
UW-SIE v2.0 introduces DMDE-USE, a coupled engine that simultaneously
evolves dark matter density perturbations and dark energy equation-of-state
parameters $w(z)$ within a single tensor field framework.

\section{Governing Equations}
\subsection{Dark Matter Potential}
The gravitational potential $\Phi$ sourced by dark matter density
perturbations $\delta\rho$ in an expanding universe is modeled via:
\[
\nabla^2 \Phi = 4\pi G a^2 \delta\rho,
\]
where $a$ is the scale factor, $G$ is Newton's constant, and
$\delta\rho$ encodes the dark matter overdensity field.

\subsection{Dark Energy Background}
The background expansion is governed by a Friedmann-like relation:
\[
H^2(t) = \frac{8\pi G}{3}\rho_{\text{tot}} + \frac{\Lambda}{3},
\]
with $\rho_{\text{tot}}$ including dark matter, baryons, radiation, and
dark energy. The dark energy component is parameterized via an
equation-of-state $w(z)$, where $z$ is redshift:
\[
p_{\text{DE}} = w(z)\,\rho_{\text{DE}}.
\]

\subsection{Coupling Strategy}
DMDE-USE couples the evolution of $\delta\rho$ and $w(z)$ by:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Updating $\Phi$ via the Poisson equation at each timestep.
    \item Recomputing $H(t)$ using the current $\rho_{\text{DE}}(z)$ and $w(z)$.
    \item Propagating particle trajectories in the evolving potential $\Phi$.
    \item Adjusting dark energy parameters dynamically under stress-test
    scenarios (phantom-like $w(z) < -1$, rapid transitions, etc.).
\end{itemize}

\section{Numerical Implementation}
\subsection{Tensor Field Solvers}
The gravitational potential and density fields are represented on a
cosmological grid as tensor fields. UW-SIE v2.0 employs:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Multi-resolution grids for filament and halo structures.
    \item Stable finite-difference or spectral solvers for $\nabla^2\Phi$.
    \item Adaptive timestep control to prevent numerical instabilities
    under extreme $w(z)$ variance.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Distributed Execution}
DMDE-USE runs across a distributed cluster:
\begin{itemize}
    \item 10{,}000 parallel nodes handling petabyte-scale tensor updates.
    \item RocksDB-based state backends for persistent checkpointing.
    \item High-concurrency synchronization protocols to avoid data loss
    during simulated cosmic expansion epochs.
\end{itemize}

\section{Stress Testing Protocol}
UW-SIE v2.0 executes the following dark matter/dark energy stress vectors:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Redshift range $z = 0$ to $z = 25$ with aggressive $w(z)$
    fluctuations.
    \item High-density filament collision scenarios in the cosmic web.
    \item Rapid transitions in dark energy behavior (e.g., quintessence
    to phantom regimes).
\end{itemize}
The performance target is stable tensor field convergence for $\Phi$
without catastrophic floating-point underflow or overflow.

\section{Results}
Under the UW-V2-STRESS-01 protocol:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The solver maintained stable convergence of $\Phi$ across all
